      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "What I was doing: nothing in a tool \u2014 I was typing the write-up and got cut off mid-sentence twice. No more silent stretches.\n\n## Why the GPU spikes instead of saturating\n\nYour numbers say this plainly: **~47 s per step, of which the GPU works ~2\u20137 s and idles ~40 s.** GPU util was 100% for one sample, then 0% for the next five. One dataloader worker was parked in `D` state \u2014 blocked on a network read.\n\nSo the GPU isn't underused because of anything GPU-side. Each sample needs **3 frames \u00d7 13 channels \u00d7 4096\u00d74096** pulled from S3 (inputs at \u221260/\u221236 min + 1 target). At `batch_size=1` with 4 workers, the loader delivers a sample every ~47 s and the GPU eats it in a few seconds. **Nothing you change on the model side will help while that's true** \u2014 bigger batches, `compile`, more AMP just make the idle gaps longer.\n\nTo saturate the GPU you'd need ~30 concurrent fetchers at 2.6 GB each. That's not happening in RAM. So the fix is to **stop fetching from S3 inside the training loop**: pre-download every file once (network-parallel, no GPU involved), then train off local disk where reads are ~9 s and workers can actually keep up.\n\nI need your cache dir and index paths to write that prefetch correctly \u2014 one quick read, that's it:",
      "type": "msg",
