    {
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Answering all of it, in the order that matters for your presentation.\n\n## 1. Yes \u2014 you can present what you already have\n\nThis is the key thing: **`lit_model` right now holds 33 steps of fine-tuning**, and your `df_baseline` (saved 18:12, before any training) is a valid untrained control. That's a complete before/after story. You do **not** need to re-run to have something to show.\n\nDo this first so an interrupt can't lose it:\n\n```python\ntrainer.save_checkpoint(\"interrupted_33steps.ckpt\")   # weights are in memory NOW\nimport wandb\nif wandb.run is not None:\n    wandb.run.finish()        # this is why wandb still says \"running\"\n```\n\nThen run Stage 4 + the plots below and label it honestly: *\"33 of 55 optimizer steps, 1 epoch, LoRA.\"* If you then have time, re-run for the full 55 \u2014 but you're not blocked.\n\n## 2. More workers: easy yes, with a ceiling\n\nOne line in cell 4: `LOADER_WORKERS = 4` \u2192 `8`. You have 16 CPUs and 87 GB free; 8 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2.6 GB \u2248 41 GB, safe. Also set `persistent_workers=True`.\n\nBut know the ceiling: your cache is on **EFS at 301 MB/s**, and each sample reads 3 frames \u00d7 564 MB \u2248 1.7 GB. So even perfectly warm and parallel, you're capped near **~6 s/step** \u2192 ~6 min for 55 steps, versus 43 min now. Workers get you there; they can't beat the filesystem. The prefetch cell I sent earlier is what removes S3 from the loop; workers then overlap the EFS reads.\n\n## 3. Checks *during* the run\n\nThe reason you had no visibility: tqdm's bar dies with the browser websocket. Plain prints don't. Add this callback to `Trainer(callbacks=[...])`:\n\n```python\nimport time, datetime as dt\n\nclass StepProgress(L.Callback):\n    \"\"\"Durable per-step progress. Also writes a file, so from a terminal:\n         tail -f downstream_apps/flare_forecast/train_progress.log\n    Watch data-wait: ~0 means the GPU is finally the bottleneck, not the network.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, logfile=\"train_progress.log\"): self.logfile = logfile\n    def _say(self, m):\n        line = f\"[{dt.datetime.now():%H:%M:%S}] {m}\"\n        print(line, flush=True)\n        with open(self.logfile, \"a\") as fh: fh.write(line + \"\\n\")\n    def on_train_start(self, tr, pl):\n        self.total = tr.max_epochs * len(tr.train_dataloader)\n        self.t0, self.prev = time.perf_counter(), None\n        self._say(f\"START {self.total} steps -> {self.logfile}\")\n    def on_train_batch_start(self, tr, pl, batch, i):\n        now = time.perf_counter()\n        self.wait = 0.0 if self.prev is None else now - self.prev\n        self.begin = now\n    def on_train_batch_end(self, tr, pl, outputs, batch, i):\n        self.prev = time.perf_counter()\n        step, el = tr.global_step, self.prev - self.t0\n        per = el / max(step, 1)\n        loss = outputs[\"loss\"] if isinstance(outputs, dict) else outputs\n        self._say(f\"step {step:3d}/{self.total}  loss {float(loss):.4f}  \"\n                  f\"compute {self.prev-self.begin:5.1f}s  data-wait {self.wait:5.1f}s  \"\n                  f\"{per:5.1f}s/step  ETA {(self.total-step)*per/60:4.1f}m\")\n    def on_train_end(self, tr, pl):\n        self._say(f\"END status={tr.state.status} step={tr.global_step}/{self.total}\")\n```\n\nNow \"is it alive?\" is answerable from a terminal without touching the kernel \u2014 which is exactly what you lacked tonight.\n\n## 4. Checks *after* `trainer.fit` \u2014 and a fix\n\nYour status cell lied to you. It printed \"max_time guard fired\" when actually **you** interrupted; the `if global_step < expected` branch blames `max_time` for any shortfall. Replace it:\n\n```python\nexpected = trainer.max_epochs * len(train_data_loader)\nstatus = str(trainer.state.status)\nprint(\"status         :\", status)\nprint(\"epochs done    :\", trainer.current_epoch, \"of\", trainer.max_epochs)\nprint(\"optimizer steps:\", trainer.global_step, \"of\", expected)\nif \"INTERRUPTED\" in status.upper():\n    print(\"  ^^ interrupted by you / kernel restart -- max_time did NOT fire\")\nelif trainer.global_step < expected:\n    print(\"  ^^ stopped early -- the max_time guard fired\")\nelse:\n    print(\"  ^^ completed all steps\")\nprint(\"checkpoint     :\", trainer.checkpoint_callback.last_model_path or \"(none written)\")\nprint(\"NOTE: save_last only writes at EPOCH end -- mid-epoch interrupts save nothing.\")\n```\n\nThree things tell you it's actually *working*, not just running: loss trending down in `train_progress.log`, `status: FINISHED` with steps matching, and **skill vs persistence > 0** in the plots below.\n\n## 5. Stage 4: numbers only \u2014 here are the images\n\nYou're right that my Stage 4 gives you **no pictures**, only tables. Your plot cell gives pictures but runs on **training** samples from `SAMPLES`. This runs on **val/test** with the fine-tuned model \u2014 add it after Stage 4:\n\n```python\n# ===== STAGE 4b: visual GT vs PREDICTION on val/test (presentation panels) =====\nimport numpy as np, pandas as pd, torch, matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport sunpy.visualization.colormaps as cm          # registers sdoaia131, hmimag, ...\nfrom torch.utils.data._utils.collate import default_collate\n\nCHANNELS_TO_PLOT = [\"aia131\", \"aia171\"]\nSTRIDE, N_SHOW, FACTOR = 4, 2, 1     # stride 4 = ~16x faster and exact; FACTOR 10 = enhanced\n\nchannel_order = cfg.data.channels\nch_idx = {c: channel_order.index(c) for c in CHANNELS_TO_PLOT}\n\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef predict(lit, item):\n    lit = lit.to(\"cuda\").eval()\n    b = default_collate([item])\n    b = {k: (v.to(\"cuda\") if torch.is_tensor(v) else v) for k, v in b.items()}\n    with torch.autocast(\"cuda\", dtype=torch.bfloat16):\n        out = lit(b)[0].float().cpu().numpy()\n    del b; torch.cuda.empty_cache()\n    return out\n\ndef show_sample(ds, idx, lit, split):\n    item = ds[idx]\n    pred_norm = predict(lit, item)\n    gt_n, now_n = item[\"forecast\"][:, 0], item[\"ts\"][:, -1]   # normalized: what loss sees\n    mse_m = float(np.mean([((pred_norm[k]-gt_n[k])**2).mean() for k in range(len(channel_order))]))\n    mse_p = float(np.mean([((now_n[k]   -gt_n[k])**2).mean() for k in range(len(channel_order))]))\n    skill = 1 - mse_m / mse_p\n\n    row, ref, S = ds.df_valid_indices.iloc[idx], pd.Timestamp(ds.valid_indices[idx]), STRIDE\n    inv, _c = ds.inverse_transform_data, np.ascontiguousarray\n    last_in = inv(_c(item[\"ts\"][:, -1, ::S, ::S]))\n    gt      = inv(_c(item[\"forecast\"][:, 0, ::S, ::S]))\n    pr      = inv(_c(pred_norm[:, ::S, ::S]))\n\n    def lim(a):\n        nz = a[a != 0]\n        return (np.percentile(nz, 99) * FACTOR) if nz.size else 1.0\n\n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(len(CHANNELS_TO_PLOT), 4,\n                             figsize=(3.3*4, 3.3*len(CHANNELS_TO_PLOT)), squeeze=False)\n    for r, ch in enumerate(CHANNELS_TO_PLOT):\n        k = ch_idx[ch]\n        vmax = lim(gt[k])          # GT and PREDICTION SHARE one scale -- essential, or a\n                                   # uniformly-too-bright prediction looks correct\n        for c, (img, label, v) in enumerate([\n                (last_in[k], \"LAST INPUT\", vmax), (gt[k], \"GROUND TRUTH\", vmax),\n                (pr[k], \"PREDICTION\", vmax), (pr[k]-gt[k], \"PREDICTION - GT\", None)]):\n            ax = axes[r][c]\n            if v is None:\n                e = np.percentile(np.abs(img), 99) * FACTOR or 1.0\n                ax.imshow(img, cmap=\"coolwarm\", vmin=-e, vmax=e)\n                txt, col = f\"{ch}  {label}\\nRMSE {np.sqrt((img**2).mean()):.4g}\", \"k\"\n            elif \"hmi\" in ch:\n                ax.imshow(img, cmap=\"hmimag\", vmin=-v, vmax=v); txt, col = f\"{ch}  {label}\", \"k\"\n            else:\n                ax.imshow(img, cmap=f\"sdo{ch}\", vmin=0, vmax=v); txt, col = f\"{ch}  {label}\", \"w\"\n            ax.text(0.01, 0.99, txt, transform=ax.transAxes, ha=\"left\", va=\"top\",\n                    fontsize=8, color=col)\n            ax.set_xticks([]); ax.set_yticks([])\n    fig.suptitle(f\"{split.upper()}  sample {idx}  {row['GOES_class']}  {ref}   |   \"\n                 f\"MSE {mse_m:.4f} vs persistence {mse_p:.4f}  ->  skill {skill:+.1%}\",\n                 fontsize=11)\n    fig.tight_layout(); plt.show()\n    return dict(split=split, i=idx, GOES_class=row[\"GOES_class\"],\n                time=str(ref)[:16], mse_model=mse_m, mse_persistence=mse_p, skill=skill)\n\nsummary = []\nfor split, dl in ((\"val\", val_data_loader), (\"test\", test_data_loader)):\n    d = dl.dataset\n    d.flip_probability = 0.0\n    for idx in range(min(N_SHOW, len(d))):\n        summary.append(show_sample(d, idx, lit_model, split))\nprint(pd.DataFrame(summary).to_string(index=False))\n```\n\n**The one number to point at on a slide:** `skill` \u2014 positive means the model beats copying the \"now\" frame forward. If it's negative, fine-tuning hasn't helped yet, and 33 steps is a fair explanation.\n\n## 6. What that scan cell does\n\nIt answers one narrow question: *is the 50/50 random flip actually firing?* It walks the training set at `flip_probability=0.5`, records each draw as `F` (flipped) or `.` (upright), and **stops as soon as it has one of each**. Those two samples become `SAMPLES`, which your plot cell then renders.\n\nTwo things to know: the two samples are **different flares** that happened to land on opposite sides of the coin \u2014 not the same flare shown both ways. And it reads full samples from disk, so on a cold cache it's slow. It's a diagnostic, not part of training \u2014 safe to skip once you trust the flip (your cell 11 check A already proves the flip is correct on both input and target).",
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789671834.7096612,
      "id": "ec9bb089-c057-422a-b597-8dc77bfe48f2",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
    },
    {
      "sender": "jpereira",
      "id": "463d28e8-b171-4f89-8577-bf1de6fed0df",
