      "id": "a77b7c0e-c1ac-4a37-bf59-a62432788a51",
      "type": "msg",
      "body": "ok what's the purpose of stage 4 cell and outputs? what about stage 4b cell and outputs? what will they be?\n\nAlso for the trial, let's make it simpler. we were gonna do 87 flares, let's just cut that to 50 or something for NOW. how do the numbers change? whats the expected run time now? can we keep ratios mostly the same? once i know it worsk for a small test we can extend it and let it run overnight.",
      "time": 1789672250.3952281
    },
    {
      "body": "## What Stage 4 is for\n\n**Stage 4 = the numbers.** It scores your val+test samples with the fine-tuned model, then stacks that against `df_baseline` (the untrained control) and prints four tables:\n\n| table | question it answers |\n|---|---|\n| `run \u00d7 orientation` | Does flipping hurt? \u2014 your hypothesis |\n| `run \u00d7 split \u00d7 orientation` | Does it hold on cycle 25 (2023 test) vs cycle 24 (val)? |\n| `run` | Did fine-tuning help at all? |\n| per-sample `flipped \u2212 upright` with mean/**std**/count | The headline, *with* its variance |\n\nThree columns in each: `error` (MSE over all 13 channels, full disk), `error_persistence` (just copy the \"now\" frame forward \u2014 the do-nothing control), `error_flare_region` (MSE on the brightest 0.1% of aia131 pixels, i.e. where the flare actually is).\n\n**Stage 4b = the pictures.** Per sample: 2 channels \u00d7 4 panels \u2014 last input, ground truth, prediction, prediction\u2212GT error \u2014 with `skill vs persistence` in the title. Stage 4 tells you *whether* it works; 4b lets you see *how* it fails.\n\n## Set your expectations now, not on stage\n\nAt 33 steps, expect `error` \u2248 0.2\u20130.35 (consistent with your `train_loss` 0.346), baseline \u2248 fine-tuned, and `flipped \u2212 upright` with a **std larger than its mean** \u2014 i.e. inconclusive at n=16. In 4b, expect the prediction to look like a slightly blurred copy of the last input, with `skill` near zero or negative. That's the honest 33-step result, and \"inconclusive at this sample size\" is a legitimate thing to present.\n\n## Cutting to ~50: change only the training set\n\nDon't cut val/test. Two reasons: it costs you the statistical power your conclusion rests on, and **it invalidates `results_baseline.csv`** \u2014 those 32 eval samples were chosen by `N_VAL`/`N_TEST`, so changing them forces you to re-run the whole baseline pass.\n\nSo in cell 4:\n\n```python\nN_TRAIN, N_VAL, N_TEST = 32, 16, 16       # 64 flares; was 55, 16, 16\n```\n\n`pick_stratified` pops deterministically from seed-42 shuffled per-year buckets, so **these 32 are a subset of your original 55** \u2014 much of their data is already in your cache. Same seed, same years, same stratification: ratios hold.\n\n| | before | trial |\n|---|---|---|\n| train steps (batch 1) | 55 | **32** |\n| eval samples | 32 | 32 (unchanged) |\n| baseline pass | done | **reuse `df_baseline`** |\n| fit time | ~43 min | **~8\u201325 min** |\n| Stage 4 | ~6 min | ~4 min (warm) |\n\nSet `max_time={\"minutes\": 60}` \u2014 comfortably above the worst case, and it won't fire if things go well.\n\nThe spread on fit time is real: cold samples cost ~47 s, cached ones ~7\u201315 s, and I don't know exactly which of the 55 got downloaded during your 33 steps. `train_progress.log`'s `data-wait` column will tell you within the first two steps which regime you're in.\n\n## Order\n\n1. Cell 4 \u2192 `32, 16, 16`, `LOADER_WORKERS = 8`\n2. Cell 14 \u2192 `max_time={\"minutes\": 60}`, add `StepProgress()`\n3. Re-run cells 5, 6, 7, 10 (fresh model \u2014 you want a clean 32-step run, not 33 + 32)\n4. Fit. Watch `tail -f train_progress.log`\n5. Stage 4 (skip Stage 1 \u2014 reuse the baseline) \u2192 Stage 4b\n\nThen for overnight: raise `N_TRAIN` only, leave `N_VAL`/`N_TEST` alone, and the baseline still stands.",
