      "time": 1789673307.9739132,
      "mentions": [],
      "body": "Fine \u2014 check it yourself in a cell:\n\n```python\nimport os, pandas as pd\nfor f in [\"results_baseline.csv\", \"results_finetuned.csv\", \"results_all.csv\"]:\n    print(f, \"->\", len(pd.read_csv(f)), \"rows\" if os.path.exists(f) else \"MISSING\")\n```\n\nTwo things the restart cost you, both recoverable:\n\n**1. `df_baseline` is gone from memory** but the file is on disk. Before Stage 4, reload it:\n\n```python\ndf_baseline = pd.read_csv(\"results_baseline.csv\")\n```\n\nOtherwise Stage 4 will `NameError` on `pd.concat([df_baseline, df_finetuned])`.\n\n**2. That 9-minute Stage 4 pass** \u2014 if `results_finetuned.csv` exists, it finished and you can reload it the same way instead of re-running. If it's missing, the restart killed it mid-pass and you'll need to redo it after this fit anyway.\n\nAlso note: `interrupted_33steps.ckpt` is still on disk, but you're about to train a fresh model, so those 33 steps are now just a fallback if tonight's run goes badly.\n\nSo the order from here:\n\n1. Cells **5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10** \u2014 rebuild the model and `lit_model`\n2. Cell **14** \u2014 the trainer (with `StepProgress` if you added it)\n3. The **fit** cell\n4. `df_baseline = pd.read_csv(\"results_baseline.csv\")`\n5. Stage 4, then Stage 4b\n\nOne check worth doing at step 1: cell 7 prints `Trainable parameters:`. It should say **5.8M** trainable of 371M, matching what your last run's model summary reported. If it says something different, LoRA didn't attach the same way and the run isn't comparable to your baseline.",
