    {
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Because Stage 1 is the heaviest cell in the notebook by design \u2014 the comment in it says so: *\"Every sample here is a cold S3 read, so this is the slowest stage.\"*\n\nIt scores **32 samples** (16 val + 16 test), each needing 3 frames \u00d7 564 MB \u2248 **1.7 GB**, so ~54 GB of reads, plus signum-log + z-score over 218M elements per frame and a forward pass. At the ~17 s/sample you measured, that's ~9 minutes; on genuinely cold S3 it was ~12.\n\n**But you shouldn't be running it at all.** You already have that result on disk from 18:12. Interrupt it and do this instead:\n\n```python\ndf_baseline = pd.read_csv(\"results_baseline.csv\")\nprint(len(df_baseline), \"rows\")\n```\n\nInterrupting is safe \u2014 `evaluate()` only writes the CSV after the whole pass, so there's no partial file to corrupt.\n\nWhy the old baseline is still valid: it must be the **untrained** model (it was \u2014 computed before any `fit`), and it must cover the **same** eval samples. You changed `N_TRAIN` to 32 but left `N_VAL`/`N_TEST` at 16, and `pick_stratified` is seeded at 42 with the same years \u2014 so val/test are the identical 32 flares. That's exactly why I told you not to touch `N_VAL`/`N_TEST`.\n\nThe one thing that *would* invalidate it: if you had rebuilt the model with different LoRA settings, or changed `N_VAL`/`N_TEST`/`SUBSET_SEED`/`VAL_YEARS`/`TEST_YEARS`. You haven't.\n\nSo: interrupt Stage 1, load the CSV, go straight to the fit cell.",
