          "id": "opus[1m]"
        },
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
      },
      "body": "clarify why the outputs for trainer.fit look like this? the ETAs keep changing like crazy?\n\nTraining: |          | 0/? [00:00<?, ?it/s]\n[19:44:23] step   1/32  loss 0.2269  compute   4.1s  data-wait   0.0s  209.1s/step  ETA 108.0m\n[19:44:52] step   2/32  loss 0.3401  compute   2.1s  data-wait  26.7s  118.9s/step  ETA 59.5m\n[19:44:55] step   3/32  loss 0.3342  compute   2.1s  data-wait   0.8s   80.3s/step  ETA 38.8m\n[19:44:58] step   4/32  loss 0.2451  compute   2.1s  data-wait   0.8s   60.9s/step  ETA 28.4m\n[19:45:01] step   5/32  loss 0.2499  compute   2.1s  data-wait   0.8s   49.3s/step  ETA 22.2m\n[19:45:04] step   6/32  loss 0.2190  compute   2.1s  data-wait   0.8s   41.6s/step  ETA 18.0m\n[19:45:07] step   7/32  loss 0.2425  compute   2.1s  data-wait   0.8s   36.1s/step  ETA 15.0m\n[19:45:10] step   8/32  loss 0.5790  compute   2.1s  data-wait   0.8s   31.9s/step  ETA 12.8m",
      "time": 1789674405.5679226,
      "id": "63b2ad89-6e50-45ab-a086-0f933f1c6b55",
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "id": "74b21649-391b-432c-b48f-9c818e93811a",
      "mentions": [],
      "time": 1789674428.9036376,
      "body": "Two separate things, and the second one is good news.\n\n## Why the ETA swings\n\nMy callback computes `per = elapsed / step` \u2014 a **cumulative average from `on_train_start`**. Your startup cost was huge: `START` at 19:40:54, step 1 at 19:44:23 = **209 seconds** of worker spawn + filling prefetch queues before a single step ran. That 209 s is baked into the average forever, so the ETA just decays as fast steps dilute it. It's not measuring your actual speed.\n\n**Read the timestamps instead.** Steps 3\u21928: 19:44:55 \u2192 19:45:10 = 15 s for 5 steps = **~3 s/step**. So 24 steps left \u2248 72 s. You'll be done around **19:46:20**, not in 12.8 minutes.\n\nThat's a flaw in my ETA, not in your run. For later, swap the cumulative average for a recent-window rate:\n\n```python\n    # in on_train_batch_end, replace: per = el / max(step, 1)\n    self.recent = getattr(self, \"recent\", [])[-9:] + [self.prev - self.begin + self.wait]\n    per = sum(self.recent) / len(self.recent)      # last 10 steps, not since startup\n```\n\n## The important number\n\n```\ncompute 2.1s    data-wait 0.8s\n```\n\n**This is exactly what you were asking for two hours ago.** Compare to your 18:14 run: ~40 s of data-wait per 2 s of compute. Now the GPU does 2.1 s of work and waits 0.8 s \u2014 the GPU is the bottleneck, which is the healthy state. 8 workers \u00d7 prefetch 2 keeps 16 samples buffered, and the frames are warm in the EFS cache, so the loader stays ahead of the GPU.\n\nYour 47 s/step became ~3 s/step. That's the ~15\u00d7 you wanted.\n\n## One thing not to over-read\n\n`0.2269, 0.3401, 0.3342, 0.2451, 0.2499, 0.2190, 0.2425, 0.5790` \u2014 still noise, no trend, and step 8 jumped to 0.579. At 32 steps of batch size 1 that's expected; individual flares vary more than the model is learning. Judge the run by Stage 4's `skill vs persistence`, not by this sequence.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "raw_time": false,
      "metadata": {
        "settings": {
          "effort": "high"
        },
        "model": {
          "settings": {
            "fast": "on"
          },
          "id": "opus[1m]"
        },
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
      },
      "id": "2306d26f-93e8-46f0-b504-93ead453a810",
      "sender": "jpereira",
      "body": "but then this one took much longer, how come? [19:45:10] step   8/32  loss 0.5790  compute   2.1s  data-wait   0.8s   31.9s/step  ETA 12.8m\n[19:47:10] step   9/32  loss 0.2055  compute   2.1s  data-wait 118.2s   41.7s/step  ETA 16.0m",
      "time": 1789674458.3783002,
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "type": "msg",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "time": 1789674483.1562529,
      "body": "Because your buffer ran dry, and step 9 hit a **cold** sample.\n\nHere's the sequence: during that 209 s startup, 8 workers \u00d7 `prefetch_factor=2` filled a queue of up to 16 ready samples. Steps 1\u20138 then drained that queue at 3 s/step \u2014 far faster than workers can refill it. Once it emptied, the loop had to wait for a worker to actually finish fetching, and that one wasn't in the cache: **118 s of real S3 download**.\n\nNote `compute` stayed at exactly 2.1 s. The GPU side is rock steady; all the variance is data.\n\n## Expect a sawtooth, not a steady rate\n\nYour 32 training flares are a **mixed cache** \u2014 some were downloaded during the interrupted 33-step run, some never were. So you'll see bursts of ~3 s steps (cached, buffer has stock) punctuated by 60\u2013120 s stalls (cold fetch, buffer empty).\n\nSteady-state throughput is set by workers, not by the fast steps:\n\n```\ncold sample \u2248 120 s \u00f7 8 workers \u2248 15 s/step\n```\n\nSo 23 steps left \u2192 roughly **6\u201311 minutes**, finishing around **19:53\u201319:58**. Your `max_time` is 60 min, so there's no risk of the guard firing.\n\n## This is why the prefetch cell exists\n\nThat 118 s stall is exactly what pre-downloading eliminates: it moves the network work *outside* the training loop, where you can run 12 threads instead of 8 workers and don't stall the GPU at all. For 32 flares tonight it's not worth adding mid-run. For the overnight run at 55+ flares, run the prefetch cell first and every step becomes the 3 s kind.\n\nNothing to do now \u2014 let it finish. The `data-wait` column is telling you the truth, which is the whole point.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "id": "f4619a6d-a326-43dc-929e-034e20c5b997",
      "mentions": []
    },
    {
      "type": "msg",
      "metadata": {
        "settings": {
          "effort": "high"
        },
        "model": {
          "id": "opus[1m]",
          "settings": {
            "fast": "on"
          }
        },
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
      },
      "id": "98ea9573-4989-47d3-8a49-3301faca7578",
      "time": 1789674574.840038,
      "sender": "jpereira",
      "body": "ok now tell me what stage 4 and stage 4b will give me again? ",
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "## Stage 4 \u2014 the numbers\n\nRuns `evaluate()` on your val + test flares with the now-trained `lit_model`, writes `results_finetuned.csv` and `results_all.csv`, then prints four tables.\n\nThree metrics in every table, all MSE in normalized space (same units as your `train_loss` \u2248 0.2\u20130.3):\n\n| column | what it is |\n|---|---|\n| `error` | model vs truth, all 13 channels, full disk |\n| `error_persistence` | **copy the \"now\" frame forward** \u2014 the do-nothing control |\n| `error_flare_region` | model vs truth on the brightest 0.1% of aia131 \u2014 where the flare is |\n\nThe four tables:\n\n1. **`run \u00d7 orientation`** \u2014 upright vs flipped, baseline vs finetuned. Your hypothesis.\n2. **`run \u00d7 split \u00d7 orientation`** \u2014 does it hold on cycle 25 (2023 test) as well as cycle 24 (val)?\n3. **`run`** \u2014 did fine-tuning help at all?\n4. **per-sample `flipped \u2212 upright`, with mean / std / count** \u2014 the headline. Positive mean = flipping hurts.\n\n**How to read it:** `error` below `error_persistence` means the model beats doing nothing. On table 4, compare mean against std \u2014 if std is bigger, you have no effect at this sample size, and that's a legitimate finding to report.\n\nRuntime ~17 s/sample, so a few minutes.\n\n## Stage 4b \u2014 the pictures\n\nFor the first 2 samples of each split, one figure each: **rows = channels** (aia131, aia171), **4 columns**:\n\n```\nLAST INPUT | GROUND TRUTH | PREDICTION | PREDICTION \u2212 GT\n```\n\nGround truth and prediction share one color scale deliberately \u2014 otherwise a prediction that's uniformly too bright looks correct. The 4th panel is the error map with its RMSE, so you see *where* it's wrong, not just how much.\n\nEach title carries `MSE ... vs persistence ... -> skill \u00b1x%`. Then a summary table of all plotted samples.\n\n**`skill` is your slide number.** Positive = beats persistence.\n\n## What to expect honestly\n\nAt 32 steps, the prediction will likely look like a slightly blurred copy of the last input, `skill` near zero, and `flipped \u2212 upright` swamped by its own std. Stage 4 tells you *whether* it works; 4b shows you *how* it fails. Both are presentable \u2014 \"inconclusive at n=32 after 1 epoch\" is a real result, and the error maps make a much better slide than a flat loss curve.",
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "1f728359-7a30-476d-927c-da0cac6f2b6d",
      "time": 1789674596.4714117,
      "mentions": []
    },
    {
      "body": "i need 4b plots to have the two inputs and structure similar to what i had before. i had i structured that way because that's what's the most helpful for our group.\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport torch\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport matplotlib.gridspec as gridspec\nimport sunpy.visualization.colormaps as cm  # registers 'sdoaia131', 'hmimag', ...\nfrom torch.utils.data._utils.collate import default_collate\n\n# ==================================================================\n# CONFIG FOR THIS CHECK\n# ==================================================================\nCHANNELS_TO_PLOT = [\"aia131\", \"aia171\"]          # one row per channel\nSCALE_VERSIONS   = [(\"normal\", 1), (\"enhanced\", 10)]   # 99th-pct multiplier\nSTRIDE           = 1     # 1 = full 4096. Set 4 to make the figure ~16x faster;\n                         # inverse_transform is elementwise, so striding is exact.\nSHARE_GT_PRED_SCALE = True   # see note under section 4\n\ntrain_dataset = train_data_loader.dataset        # nb0's name for it\nidx           = 0\n\nchannel_order = cfg.data.channels\nch_idx = {c: channel_order.index(c) for c in CHANNELS_TO_PLOT}\n\nitem = train_dataset[idx]\n\nn_inputs = item[\"ts\"].shape[1]\nn_lead   = item[\"forecast\"].shape[1]\nassert n_lead == 1, f\"this plot assumes one target frame; got {n_lead}. Set rollout_steps: 0.\"\n\n# ==================================================================\n# 0. RUN THE MODEL  (NEW)\n# ==================================================================\n# lit_model, not model: lit_model wraps the PEFT model with the trained adapters\n# and head. Lightning moves it back to CPU after fit, hence the explicit .to().\nlit_model = lit_model.to(\"cuda\").eval()\nwith torch.no_grad(), torch.autocast(\"cuda\", dtype=torch.bfloat16):\n    _b = default_collate([item])\n    _b = {k: (v.to(\"cuda\") if torch.is_tensor(v) else v) for k, v in _b.items()}\n    pred_norm = lit_model(_b)[0].float().cpu().numpy()   # (13, H, W), normalized space\ndel _b; torch.cuda.empty_cache()\n\n# ==================================================================\n# 1. TRUE FRAME TIMESTAMPS  (from the sample itself, not from ds_index)\n# ==================================================================\nreference_timestep = pd.Timestamp(train_dataset.valid_indices[idx])\n\n# helio.py: the LAST entry of the sorted input offsets is the \"now\" frame.\nnow_offset_min = sorted(cfg.data.time_delta_input_minutes)[-1]\nnow_time = reference_timestep + pd.Timedelta(minutes=now_offset_min)\n\n# time_delta_input = hours BEFORE now ; lead_time_delta = hours before now (neg = future)\ninput_times  = [now_time - pd.Timedelta(minutes=round(float(h) * 60))\n                for h in item[\"time_delta_input\"]]\ntarget_times = [now_time - pd.Timedelta(minutes=round(float(h) * 60))\n                for h in item[\"lead_time_delta\"]]\n\n# ==================================================================\n# 2. PROVENANCE REPORT\n# ==================================================================\nrow = train_dataset.df_valid_indices.iloc[idx]\nanchor_col   = cfg.data.ds_time_column\nanchor_event = pd.Timestamp(item[\"ds_index\"])\ngt_time      = target_times[0]\ngap          = abs(gt_time - anchor_event)\n\nprint(f\"FLARE EVENT      : {row['GOES_class']}   \")\nprint(f\"  start_time     : {row['start_time']}\")\nprint(f\"  peak_time      : {row['peak_time']}\")\nprint(f\"  end_time       : {row['end_time']}\")\nprint(' ')\nprint(' ')\nprint(f\"ds_time_column = '{anchor_col}'  ->  {anchor_event}\")\nprint(' ')\nprint(f\"Ground Truth {gt_time}\")\nprint(f\"Reference Timestamp (helio.py anchor): {reference_timestep}\")\nprint(f\"    offset from {anchor_col:<11}: {gap}  \"\n      f\"({gap.total_seconds()/60:.0f} min)\")\nprint(f\"    ds_index vs reference gap: {abs(reference_timestep - anchor_event)}\")\nprint(' ')\nprint(' ')\nprint(\"config: \", cfg.data.time_delta_input_minutes,\n      f\"  | n_input_timestamps = {train_dataset.n_input_timestamps}\")\nprint(' ')\nprint(\"source files:\")\nfor k, ts in enumerate(input_times):\n    print(f\"  [input {k}] {ts}  (t{'' if k==0 else ''}={(ts-now_time).total_seconds()/60:+.0f} min from now)\"\n          f\"\\n            -> {train_dataset.index.loc[ts, 'path']}\")\nfor k, ts in enumerate(target_times):\n    print(f\"  [GT    {k}] {ts}  ({(ts-now_time).total_seconds()/60:+.0f} min from now)\"\n          f\"\\n            -> {train_dataset.index.loc[ts, 'path']}\")\nprint(' ')\nprint(f\"Flipped?  ->  flare_dataset = {item.get('was_flipped')} \"\n      f\"| helio = {getattr(train_dataset, 'random_vert_flip', None)}\")\nprint(' ')\n\n# ==================================================================\n# 2b. PREDICTION QUALITY  (NEW)\n# ==================================================================\n# Per-channel means, then averaged: identical to the global mean (equal sizes) but\n# holds only one 67 MB channel at a time instead of four 872 MB arrays.\n_gt_n  = item[\"forecast\"][:, 0]      # normalized space -- what the loss actually sees\n_now_n = item[\"ts\"][:, -1]           # persistence: copy the \"now\" frame forward\nmse_model = float(np.mean([((pred_norm[k] - _gt_n[k]) ** 2).mean()\n                           for k in range(len(channel_order))]))\nmse_persist = float(np.mean([((_now_n[k] - _gt_n[k]) ** 2).mean()\n                             for k in range(len(channel_order))]))\nprint(\"NORMALIZED-SPACE MSE  (all 13 channels -- comparable to train_loss/val_loss)\")\nprint(f\"  model              : {mse_model:.5f}\")\nprint(f\"  persistence        : {mse_persist:.5f}   <- copy 'now' forward, no model\")\nprint(f\"  skill vs persist.  : {1 - mse_model/mse_persist:+.1%}   (>0 = model helps)\")\nprint(' ')\nprint(\"PER-CHANNEL RMSE, physical units\")\nprint(f\"  {'channel':<10} {'model':>12} {'persistence':>14}\")\nfor c, n in ch_idx.items():\n    rm = float(np.sqrt(((pred_norm[n] - _gt_n[n]) ** 2).mean()))\n    rp = float(np.sqrt(((_now_n[n]    - _gt_n[n]) ** 2).mean()))\n    print(f\"  {c:<10} {rm:>12.4g} {rp:>14.4g}\")\nprint(' ')\nprint(' ')\n\n# ==================================================================\n# 3. PHYSICAL-UNIT FRAMES PER CHANNEL\n# ==================================================================\n# Transform each FRAME once (all 13 channels at a time -- inverse_transform_data\n# indexes per-channel stats, so the channel axis must stay whole), then slice the\n# channels we plot out of the result.\ninv, _c, S = train_dataset.inverse_transform_data, np.ascontiguousarray, STRIDE\nins_phys  = [inv(_c(item[\"ts\"][:, t, ::S, ::S]))       for t in range(n_inputs)]\ntgts_phys = [inv(_c(item[\"forecast\"][:, l, ::S, ::S])) for l in range(n_lead)]\nprds_phys = [inv(_c(pred_norm[:, ::S, ::S]))]\n\nframes = {c: ([a[n] for a in ins_phys],\n              [a[n] for a in tgts_phys],\n              [a[n] for a in prds_phys]) for c, n in ch_idx.items()}\n\n# ==================================================================\n# 4. PLOTTING\n# ==================================================================\ndef _lim(a, factor):\n    nz = a[a != 0]\n    return (np.percentile(nz, 99) * factor) if nz.size else 1.0\n\ndef _show(ax, img, channel, label, factor, lim=None):\n    # lim=None keeps nb0's behaviour (each panel scaled to itself). Pass an explicit\n    # lim to force two panels onto one scale -- required for GT vs PREDICTION, or a\n    # prediction that is uniformly 10x too bright looks identical to the truth.\n    if lim is None:\n        lim = _lim(img, factor)\n    if \"hmi\" not in channel:\n        ax.imshow(img, cmap=f\"sdo{channel}\", vmin=0, vmax=lim)\n        fc = \"w\"\n    else:\n        fc = \"k\"\n        ax.imshow(img, cmap=\"coolwarm\" if \"_v\" in channel else \"hmimag\",\n                  vmin=-lim, vmax=lim)\n    ax.text(0.01, 0.99, label, transform=ax.transAxes, ha=\"left\", va=\"top\",\n            color=fc, fontsize=7)\n    ax.set_xticks([]); ax.set_yticks([])\n\nn_out   = 2 * n_lead                                  # GT columns + PRED columns\nn_cols  = n_inputs + 1 + n_out                        # +1 = spacer column\nw_ratios = [1] * n_inputs + [0.10] + [1] * n_out\n\nfor tag, factor in SCALE_VERSIONS:\n    n_rows = len(CHANNELS_TO_PLOT) * 2                # image rows + diff rows\n    panel = 3.4                                    # inches per (square) panel\n    title_in = 0.42                                # inches reserved for suptitle\n    fig_w = panel * (n_inputs + n_out) + panel * w_ratios[n_inputs]\n    fig_h = panel * n_rows + title_in\n    fig = plt.figure(figsize=(fig_w, fig_h), dpi=110)\n    gs = gridspec.GridSpec(n_rows, n_cols, figure=fig,\n                           width_ratios=w_ratios,\n                           wspace=0.02, hspace=0.03,\n                           left=0.004, right=0.996,\n                           top=1 - title_in / fig_h,   # <- kills the title gap\n                           bottom=0.004)\n    # ---- image rows: one per channel, columns = time ----\n    for r, c in enumerate(CHANNELS_TO_PLOT):\n        ins, tgts, prds = frames[c]\n        for t in range(n_inputs):\n            _show(fig.add_subplot(gs[r, t]), ins[t], c,\n                  f\"{c}\\n{input_times[t]}\\nINPUT {t}\", factor)\n        for l in range(n_lead):\n            gt_lim = _lim(tgts[l], factor)\n            _show(fig.add_subplot(gs[r, n_inputs + 1 + l]), tgts[l], c,\n                  f\"{c}\\n{target_times[l]}\\nGROUND TRUTH\", factor)\n            _show(fig.add_subplot(gs[r, n_inputs + 1 + n_lead + l]), prds[l], c,\n                  f\"{c}\\n{target_times[l]}\\nPREDICTION\", factor,\n                  lim=gt_lim if SHARE_GT_PRED_SCALE else None)\n    # ---- diff rows: centered below, layout of grid above untouched ----\n    for r, c in enumerate(CHANNELS_TO_PLOT):\n        ins, tgts, prds = frames[c]\n        diffs = []\n        if n_inputs >= 2:\n            diffs.append((ins[-1] - ins[-2],\n                          f\"{c}  \u0394 inputs\\n{input_times[-1]} \u2212 {input_times[-2]}\", None))\n        # The signal the model has to produce...\n        d_signal = tgts[0] - ins[-1]\n        lim_sig = np.percentile(np.abs(d_signal), 99) * factor or 1.0\n        diffs.append((d_signal,\n                      f\"{c}  \u0394 to ground truth\\n{target_times[0]} \u2212 {input_times[-1]}\", lim_sig))\n        # ...and how far off it is, on the SAME scale. Same amplitude as the panel to\n        # its left => the model captured none of the change.\n        diffs.append((prds[0] - tgts[0],\n                      f\"{c}  \u0394 prediction error\\nPREDICTION \u2212 GROUND TRUTH\", lim_sig))\n\n        gap_u = w_ratios[n_inputs]\n        n_d   = len(diffs)\n        side  = (sum(w_ratios) - (n_d + gap_u * (n_d - 1))) / 2\n        ratios = [side]\n        for k in range(n_d):\n            if k:\n                ratios.append(gap_u)\n            ratios.append(1)\n        ratios.append(side)\n        inner = gs[len(CHANNELS_TO_PLOT) + r, :].subgridspec(\n            1, len(ratios), width_ratios=ratios, wspace=0)\n        for k, (d, title, forced) in enumerate(diffs):\n            ax = fig.add_subplot(inner[0, 2 * k + 1])\n            lim = forced if forced is not None else (np.percentile(np.abs(d), 99) * factor or 1.0)\n            im = ax.imshow(d, cmap=\"coolwarm\", vmin=-lim, vmax=lim)\n            ax.text(0.01, 0.99, f\"{title}\\nRMSE {np.sqrt((d**2).mean()):.4g}\",\n                    transform=ax.transAxes, ha=\"left\", va=\"top\",\n                    fontsize=7, color=\"k\",\n                    bbox=dict(fc=\"w\", ec=\"none\", alpha=0.65, pad=1.5))\n            ax.set_xticks([]); ax.set_yticks([])\n            cax = ax.inset_axes([1.015, 0.0, 0.022, 1.0])\n            cb = fig.colorbar(im, cax=cax)\n            cb.ax.tick_params(labelsize=6)\n    fig.suptitle(\n        f\"[{tag} scaling, 99th pct \u00d7 {factor}]   {row['GOES_class']}   \"\n        f\"anchor='{anchor_col}' @ {anchor_event}   |   GT frame {gt_time} \"\n        f\"(gap {gap.total_seconds()/60:.0f} min)   |   label={float(item['ground_truth']):.4g}\"\n        f\"   |   MSE {mse_model:.4f} vs persistence {mse_persist:.4f}\",\n        fontsize=10, y=0.999)\n    plt.show()",
      "id": "ed499f9c-aff1-4a73-97e9-4c5b9e16f521",
      "raw_time": false,
      "time": 1789674733.0920067,
      "metadata": {
        "settings": {
          "effort": "high"
        },
        "model": {
          "id": "opus[1m]",
          "settings": {
            "fast": "on"
          }
        },
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
      },
      "sender": "jpereira",
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "id": "f9a60286-0477-4ea4-aae3-6f8b37e97bda",
      "type": "msg",
      "raw_time": false,
      "mentions": [],
      "time": 1789674826.5668318,
      "body": "Keeping your structure exactly \u2014 2 input columns, provenance report, both scalings, the three diff panels. Only the **driver** changes: it loops over val/test samples with the fine-tuned model instead of the two `SAMPLES` from the training set.\n\n```python\n# ===== STAGE 4b: provenance + GT/PREDICTION panels, driven from val/test ======\n# Identical layout to your training-set check: image rows are\n#   INPUT 0 | INPUT 1 | (spacer) | GROUND TRUTH | PREDICTION\n# and the diff rows below are  \u0394 inputs | \u0394 to ground truth | \u0394 prediction error,\n# rendered at both scalings. What changed is only where the samples come from:\n# val/test with the fine-tuned model, instead of SAMPLES from the training set.\n\nimport numpy as np, pandas as pd, torch\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt, matplotlib.gridspec as gridspec\nimport sunpy.visualization.colormaps as cm  # registers 'sdoaia131', 'hmimag', ...\nfrom torch.utils.data._utils.collate import default_collate\n\n# ==================================================================\n# CONFIG FOR THIS CHECK\n# ==================================================================\nCHANNELS_TO_PLOT = [\"aia131\", \"aia171\"]                # one row per channel\nSCALE_VERSIONS   = [(\"normal\", 1), (\"enhanced\", 10)]   # 99th-pct multiplier\nSTRIDE           = 4     # 1 = full 4096, but that is ~872 MB per inverse-transformed\n                         # frame x 4 frames per sample. Use 4 for a multi-sample sweep\n                         # and drop to 1 for the one or two figures you actually show.\nSHARE_GT_PRED_SCALE = True\nN_SHOW           = 2       # samples per split\nSHOW_FLIPPED     = False   # True = render each sample a second time, hand-flipped\n\nchannel_order = cfg.data.channels\nch_idx = {c: channel_order.index(c) for c in CHANNELS_TO_PLOT}\n\n\ndef _lim(a, factor):\n    nz = a[a != 0]\n    return (np.percentile(nz, 99) * factor) if nz.size else 1.0\n\n\ndef _show(ax, img, channel, label, factor, lim=None):\n    # lim=None scales each panel to itself. Pass an explicit lim to force two panels\n    # onto one scale -- required for GT vs PREDICTION, or a prediction that is\n    # uniformly 10x too bright looks identical to the truth.\n    if lim is None:\n        lim = _lim(img, factor)\n    if \"hmi\" not in channel:\n        ax.imshow(img, cmap=f\"sdo{channel}\", vmin=0, vmax=lim)\n        fc = \"w\"\n    else:\n        fc = \"k\"\n        ax.imshow(img, cmap=\"coolwarm\" if \"_v\" in channel else \"hmimag\",\n                  vmin=-lim, vmax=lim)\n    ax.text(0.01, 0.99, label, transform=ax.transAxes, ha=\"left\", va=\"top\",\n            color=fc, fontsize=7)\n    ax.set_xticks([]); ax.set_yticks([])\n\n\ndef report_and_plot(ds, idx, split, lit, orient=\"UPRIGHT\"):\n    item = ds[idx]\n\n    if orient == \"FLIPPED\":\n        # Flip input AND target together -- the same contract the dataset's own flip\n        # honours. Flipping only the input would ask the model for a mirror image.\n        item = dict(item)\n        item[\"ts\"]       = np.ascontiguousarray(np.flip(item[\"ts\"], axis=-2))\n        item[\"forecast\"] = np.ascontiguousarray(np.flip(item[\"forecast\"], axis=-2))\n\n    n_inputs = item[\"ts\"].shape[1]\n    n_lead   = item[\"forecast\"].shape[1]\n    assert n_lead == 1, f\"this plot assumes one target frame; got {n_lead}. Set rollout_steps: 0.\"\n\n    # ==================================================================\n    # 0. RUN THE MODEL\n    # ==================================================================\n    # lit_model, not model: lit_model wraps the PEFT model with the trained adapters\n    # and head. Lightning moves it back to CPU after fit, hence the explicit .to().\n    lit = lit.to(\"cuda\").eval()\n    with torch.no_grad(), torch.autocast(\"cuda\", dtype=torch.bfloat16):\n        _b = default_collate([item])\n        _b = {k: (v.to(\"cuda\") if torch.is_tensor(v) else v) for k, v in _b.items()}\n        pred_norm = lit(_b)[0].float().cpu().numpy()   # (13, H, W), normalized space\n    del _b; torch.cuda.empty_cache()\n\n    # ==================================================================\n    # 1. TRUE FRAME TIMESTAMPS  (from the sample itself, not from ds_index)\n    # ==================================================================\n    reference_timestep = pd.Timestamp(ds.valid_indices[idx])\n\n    # helio.py: the LAST entry of the sorted input offsets is the \"now\" frame.\n    now_offset_min = sorted(cfg.data.time_delta_input_minutes)[-1]\n    now_time = reference_timestep + pd.Timedelta(minutes=now_offset_min)\n\n    input_times  = [now_time - pd.Timedelta(minutes=round(float(h) * 60))\n                    for h in item[\"time_delta_input\"]]\n    target_times = [now_time - pd.Timedelta(minutes=round(float(h) * 60))\n                    for h in item[\"lead_time_delta\"]]\n\n    # ==================================================================\n    # 2. PROVENANCE REPORT\n    # ==================================================================\n    row = ds.df_valid_indices.iloc[idx]\n    anchor_col   = cfg.data.ds_time_column\n    anchor_event = pd.Timestamp(item[\"ds_index\"])\n    gt_time      = target_times[0]\n    gap          = abs(gt_time - anchor_event)\n\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 100)\n    print(f\"{split.upper()}  sample {idx}  |  rendered {orient}\")\n    print(\"=\" * 100)\n    print(f\"FLARE EVENT      : {row['GOES_class']}   \")\n    print(f\"  start_time     : {row['start_time']}\")\n    print(f\"  peak_time      : {row['peak_time']}\")\n    print(f\"  end_time       : {row['end_time']}\")\n    print(' ')\n    print(f\"ds_time_column = '{anchor_col}'  ->  {anchor_event}\")\n    print(' ')\n    print(f\"Ground Truth {gt_time}\")\n    print(f\"Reference Timestamp (helio.py anchor): {reference_timestep}\")\n    print(f\"    offset from {anchor_col:<11}: {gap}  ({gap.total_seconds()/60:.0f} min)\")\n    print(f\"    ds_index vs reference gap: {abs(reference_timestep - anchor_event)}\")\n    print(' ')\n    print(\"config: \", cfg.data.time_delta_input_minutes,\n          f\"  | n_input_timestamps = {ds.n_input_timestamps}\")\n    print(' ')\n    print(\"source files:\")\n    for k, ts in enumerate(input_times):\n        print(f\"  [input {k}] {ts}  (t={(ts-now_time).total_seconds()/60:+.0f} min from now)\"\n              f\"\\n            -> {ds.index.loc[ts, 'path']}\")\n    for k, ts in enumerate(target_times):\n        print(f\"  [GT    {k}] {ts}  ({(ts-now_time).total_seconds()/60:+.0f} min from now)\"\n              f\"\\n            -> {ds.index.loc[ts, 'path']}\")\n    print(' ')\n    # was_flipped is the DATASET's coin (0.0 here, so False). orient is what WE rendered.\n    print(f\"Flipped?  ->  rendered = {orient} | dataset coin = {item.get('was_flipped')} \"\n          f\"| flip_probability = {ds.flip_probability}\")\n    print(' ')\n\n    # ==================================================================\n    # 2b. PREDICTION QUALITY\n    # ==================================================================\n    _gt_n  = item[\"forecast\"][:, 0]      # normalized space -- what the loss actually sees\n    _now_n = item[\"ts\"][:, -1]           # persistence: copy the \"now\" frame forward\n    mse_model = float(np.mean([((pred_norm[k] - _gt_n[k]) ** 2).mean()\n                               for k in range(len(channel_order))]))\n    mse_persist = float(np.mean([((_now_n[k] - _gt_n[k]) ** 2).mean()\n                                 for k in range(len(channel_order))]))\n    print(\"NORMALIZED-SPACE MSE  (all 13 channels -- comparable to train_loss/val_loss)\")\n    print(f\"  model              : {mse_model:.5f}\")\n    print(f\"  persistence        : {mse_persist:.5f}   <- copy 'now' forward, no model\")\n    print(f\"  skill vs persist.  : {1 - mse_model/mse_persist:+.1%}   (>0 = model helps)\")\n    print(' ')\n    print(\"PER-CHANNEL RMSE, physical units\")\n    print(f\"  {'channel':<10} {'model':>12} {'persistence':>14}\")\n    for c, n in ch_idx.items():\n        rm = float(np.sqrt(((pred_norm[n] - _gt_n[n]) ** 2).mean()))\n        rp = float(np.sqrt(((_now_n[n]    - _gt_n[n]) ** 2).mean()))\n        print(f\"  {c:<10} {rm:>12.4g} {rp:>14.4g}\")\n    print(' ')\n\n    # ==================================================================\n    # 3. PHYSICAL-UNIT FRAMES PER CHANNEL\n    # ==================================================================\n    inv, _c, S = ds.inverse_transform_data, np.ascontiguousarray, STRIDE\n    ins_phys  = [inv(_c(item[\"ts\"][:, t, ::S, ::S]))       for t in range(n_inputs)]\n    tgts_phys = [inv(_c(item[\"forecast\"][:, l, ::S, ::S])) for l in range(n_lead)]\n    prds_phys = [inv(_c(pred_norm[:, ::S, ::S]))]\n\n    frames = {c: ([a[n] for a in ins_phys],\n                  [a[n] for a in tgts_phys],\n                  [a[n] for a in prds_phys]) for c, n in ch_idx.items()}\n\n    # ==================================================================\n    # 4. PLOTTING\n    # ==================================================================\n    n_out    = 2 * n_lead                        # GT columns + PRED columns\n    n_cols   = n_inputs + 1 + n_out              # +1 = spacer column\n    w_ratios = [1] * n_inputs + [0.10] + [1] * n_out\n\n    for tag, factor in SCALE_VERSIONS:\n        n_rows   = len(CHANNELS_TO_PLOT) * 2     # image rows + diff rows\n        panel    = 3.4                           # inches per (square) panel\n        title_in = 0.42                          # inches reserved for suptitle\n        fig_w = panel * (n_inputs + n_out) + panel * w_ratios[n_inputs]\n        fig_h = panel * n_rows + title_in\n        fig = plt.figure(figsize=(fig_w, fig_h), dpi=110)\n        gs = gridspec.GridSpec(n_rows, n_cols, figure=fig,\n                               width_ratios=w_ratios,\n                               wspace=0.02, hspace=0.03,\n                               left=0.004, right=0.996,\n                               top=1 - title_in / fig_h,\n                               bottom=0.004)\n        # ---- image rows: one per channel, columns = time ----\n        for r, c in enumerate(CHANNELS_TO_PLOT):\n            ins, tgts, prds = frames[c]\n            for t in range(n_inputs):\n                _show(fig.add_subplot(gs[r, t]), ins[t], c,\n                      f\"{c}\\n{input_times[t]}\\nINPUT {t}\", factor)\n            for l in range(n_lead):\n                gt_lim = _lim(tgts[l], factor)\n                _show(fig.add_subplot(gs[r, n_inputs + 1 + l]), tgts[l], c,\n                      f\"{c}\\n{target_times[l]}\\nGROUND TRUTH\", factor)\n                _show(fig.add_subplot(gs[r, n_inputs + 1 + n_lead + l]), prds[l], c,\n                      f\"{c}\\n{target_times[l]}\\nPREDICTION\", factor,\n                      lim=gt_lim if SHARE_GT_PRED_SCALE else None)\n        # ---- diff rows: centered below, layout of grid above untouched ----\n        for r, c in enumerate(CHANNELS_TO_PLOT):\n            ins, tgts, prds = frames[c]\n            diffs = []\n            if n_inputs >= 2:\n                diffs.append((ins[-1] - ins[-2],\n                              f\"{c}  \u0394 inputs\\n{input_times[-1]} \u2212 {input_times[-2]}\", None))\n            # The signal the model has to produce...\n            d_signal = tgts[0] - ins[-1]\n            lim_sig = np.percentile(np.abs(d_signal), 99) * factor or 1.0\n            diffs.append((d_signal,\n                          f\"{c}  \u0394 to ground truth\\n{target_times[0]} \u2212 {input_times[-1]}\", lim_sig))\n            # ...and how far off it is, on the SAME scale. Same amplitude as the panel\n            # to its left => the model captured none of the change.\n            diffs.append((prds[0] - tgts[0],\n                          f\"{c}  \u0394 prediction error\\nPREDICTION \u2212 GROUND TRUTH\", lim_sig))\n\n            gap_u  = w_ratios[n_inputs]\n            n_d    = len(diffs)\n            side   = (sum(w_ratios) - (n_d + gap_u * (n_d - 1))) / 2\n            ratios = [side]\n            for k in range(n_d):\n                if k:\n                    ratios.append(gap_u)\n                ratios.append(1)\n            ratios.append(side)\n            inner = gs[len(CHANNELS_TO_PLOT) + r, :].subgridspec(\n                1, len(ratios), width_ratios=ratios, wspace=0)\n            for k, (d, title, forced) in enumerate(diffs):\n                ax = fig.add_subplot(inner[0, 2 * k + 1])\n                lim = forced if forced is not None else (np.percentile(np.abs(d), 99) * factor or 1.0)\n                im = ax.imshow(d, cmap=\"coolwarm\", vmin=-lim, vmax=lim)\n                ax.text(0.01, 0.99, f\"{title}\\nRMSE {np.sqrt((d**2).mean()):.4g}\",\n                        transform=ax.transAxes, ha=\"left\", va=\"top\",\n                        fontsize=7, color=\"k\",\n                        bbox=dict(fc=\"w\", ec=\"none\", alpha=0.65, pad=1.5))\n                ax.set_xticks([]); ax.set_yticks([])\n                cax = ax.inset_axes([1.015, 0.0, 0.022, 1.0])\n                cb = fig.colorbar(im, cax=cax)\n                cb.ax.tick_params(labelsize=6)\n        fig.suptitle(\n            f\"{split.upper()} sample {idx}  {orient}   [{tag} scaling, 99th pct \u00d7 {factor}]   \"\n            f\"{row['GOES_class']}   anchor='{anchor_col}' @ {anchor_event}   |   \"\n            f\"GT frame {gt_time} (gap {gap.total_seconds()/60:.0f} min)   |   \"\n            f\"label={float(item['ground_truth']):.4g}   |   \"\n            f\"MSE {mse_model:.4f} vs persistence {mse_persist:.4f}\",\n            fontsize=10, y=0.999)\n        plt.show()\n        plt.close(fig)      # a multi-sample sweep otherwise holds every figure in RAM\n\n    del frames, ins_phys, tgts_phys, prds_phys, pred_norm, item\n    return dict(split=split, i=idx, orientation=orient, GOES_class=row[\"GOES_class\"],\n                time=str(reference_timestep)[:16], mse_model=mse_model,\n                mse_persistence=mse_persist, skill=1 - mse_model / mse_persist)\n\n\n# ==================================================================\n# DRIVER\n# ==================================================================\nsummary = []\nfor split, dl in ((\"val\", val_data_loader), (\"test\", test_data_loader)):\n    d = dl.dataset\n    d.flip_probability = 0.0          # orientation is rendered explicitly below\n    for idx in range(min(N_SHOW, len(d))):\n        for o in ([\"UPRIGHT\", \"FLIPPED\"] if SHOW_FLIPPED else [\"UPRIGHT\"]):\n            summary.append(report_and_plot(d, idx, split, lit_model, o))\n\nprint(\"\\n\" + pd.DataFrame(summary).to_string(index=False))\n```\n\nFour things I changed and why:\n\n- **`train_dataset` \u2192 `ds` parameter**, so the same code serves val and test. Everything derived from it (`valid_indices`, `df_valid_indices`, `index`, `inverse_transform_data`, `n_input_timestamps`) follows.\n- **`STRIDE` defaults to 4, not 1.** At stride 1, each `inverse_transform_data` call yields 13\u00d74096\u00d74096 float32 \u2248 872 MB, and you hold four per sample. Fine for one hero figure, not for a sweep. Set it to 1 for the specific figure you put on the slide.\n- **`plt.close(fig)`** after each `show()`. Your original rendered two figures and stopped; a loop over 4 samples \u00d7 2 scalings keeps 8 in memory otherwise.\n- **Dropped the `was_flipped` assert.** Orientation is now explicit via `orient`, and on val/test `flip_probability` is 0.0 so the dataset's coin is always `False`. The provenance line prints both so the distinction stays visible.\n\nSet `SHOW_FLIPPED = True` when you want the upright/flipped pair side by side for the group \u2014 that gives you the same visual comparison your original produced from `SAMPLES`, but on held-out data and with the same flare in both orientations rather than two different flares."
    },
