      "body": "for the testing data set: each image stack gets flipped then scored, then not flipped and scored? there's two scoring? is that correct or is it just like the training where its just random?"
    },
    {
      "id": "2ba756b6-84c1-4367-9bf2-aaad29c2c8a1",
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "**As you ran it: just one scoring, random \u2014 same as training.** Not two.\n\nTwo modes, and the default is the one you used:\n\n| | `paired=False` (default \u2014 what you ran) | `paired=True` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `flip_probability` on test | 0.5 | 0.0 |\n| who decides orientation | the dataset's coin, per sample | you do, explicitly |\n| scorings per image stack | **1** | **2** (upright *and* flipped) |\n| `orientation` column | records how it landed | one row of each |\n| comparison is | between *different* flares | within the *same* flare |\n\nSo right now each test stack got flipped-or-not by a coin, then scored once, and that's it. Exactly the training regime.\n\n**You can confirm it from your own output.** `evaluate()` prints a line at the end of the pass:\n\n```\n  32 samples read, 32 scores, 15 flipped / 17 upright\n```\n\nIf `scores == samples read`, it was single-scored (`paired=False`). If `scores == 2 \u00d7  samples read`, it was paired. Check that line in your Stage 4 output \u2014 it settles the question without re-reading any code.\n\nThe distinction matters for exactly one reason: your hypothesis is *\"does flipping degrade the model?\"* Answering that by comparing flipped flare A against upright flare B confounds orientation with flare difficulty. `paired=True` compares flare A to itself, which is what isolates the flip.",
