    {
      "type": "msg",
      "mentions": [],
      "time": 1789675470.65366,
      "raw_time": false,
      "id": "e4743309-52e1-4290-91a9-413744cdb797",
      "body": "Good news \u2014 **everything you need survived**:\n\n| file | when | what it is |\n|---|---|---|\n| `finetuned_32steps.ckpt` | 19:59 | your trained 32-step model (you did run the save cell) |\n| `last.ckpt` | 19:55 | same weights, written automatically at epoch end |\n| `results_baseline.csv` | 19:39 | baseline for your *current* val/test split \u2014 still valid |\n| `results_finetuned.csv` | 19:14 | **stale** \u2014 from the old 33-step model, will be overwritten |\n\n## Recovering Stage 4 / 4b\n\nYou don't need `trainer` at all \u2014 `evaluate()` and the plots only use `lit_model`, the loaders, and `cfg`. **Skip cell 14** (it would also start a pointless new wandb run).\n\n1. Run cells **1, 2, 3, 4** \u2014 and do *not* change `N_TRAIN/N_VAL/N_TEST`, `SUBSET_SEED`, `VAL_YEARS`, `TEST_YEARS`. If those change, your val/test flares change and `results_baseline.csv` no longer matches.\n2. Run cells **5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10** \u2014 this rebuilds the architecture and an *untrained* `lit_model`.\n3. Load your trained weights into it:\n\n```python\nckpt = torch.load(\"finetuned_32steps.ckpt\", map_location=\"cpu\", weights_only=False)\nlit_model.load_state_dict(ckpt[\"state_dict\"])   # strict: architecture must match exactly\nprint(\"epoch:\", ckpt.get(\"epoch\"), \" global_step:\", ckpt.get(\"global_step\"))   # expect 1, 32\n```\n\n4. **Verify you actually loaded trained weights** \u2014 this is the real check you've been asking for. PEFT initializes every `lora_B` to exactly zero, so nonzero B proves training happened and loaded:\n\n```python\nbs = [p for n, p in lit_model.named_parameters() if \"lora_B\" in n]\nmx = max(float(p.abs().max()) for p in bs)\nprint(f\"{len(bs)} lora_B tensors, max|B| = {mx:.3e}  ->\",\n      \"TRAINED\" if mx > 0 else \"UNTRAINED (B still all zeros)\")\n```\n\n5. Reload the baseline and check which mode it used, so the fine-tuned pass matches:\n\n```python\ndf_baseline = pd.read_csv(\"results_baseline.csv\")\nprint(df_baseline.groupby([\"split\", \"i\"]).size().value_counts())   # 1 = unpaired, 2 = paired\nprint(df_baseline.groupby([\"split\", \"orientation\"]).size())\n```\n\n6. Stage 4, then Stage 4b.\n\n## Where validation and testing actually happen \u2014 you're not wrong\n\n**Nowhere during `fit`.** Lightning told you this explicitly in the fit output:\n\n> `You defined a validation_step but have no val_dataloader. Skipping val loop.`\n\nCell 14 sets `limit_val_batches=0`, and you call `trainer.fit(lit_model, train_data_loader)` with no val loader. So:\n\n- The `val_loss` and `val_metrics` objects from cell 8 are **never computed**.\n- `ModelCheckpoint(save_top_k=0, save_last=True)` has **no monitor**, so validation isn't selecting a model either.\n\nVal and test are used in exactly **one** place: the `evaluate()` calls. Stage 1 scores them with the untrained model, Stage 4 with the fine-tuned one. That's your entire held-out evaluation.\n\nSo your mental model is correct: you trained, and the val/test scoring is a separate post-hoc pass. That was deliberate \u2014 at 1 epoch there's no meaningful \"best epoch\" to select, and a mid-training val pass would re-read 1.7 GB samples for a number you couldn't act on. The comment in cell 14 says as much.\n\nThe consequence worth knowing: **test (2023, cycle 25) is your only real generalization number**, and it gets touched exactly once per `evaluate()` call. Don't iterate on it.",
