      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "Checked your notebook \u2014 here's exactly what's what.\n\n## Yes, Stage 4 is the testing. So is Stage 1.\n\nValidation and testing happen in **exactly two places**, and both are `evaluate()` calls:\n\n| | Cell | Model | What it is |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Stage 1** | 14 | stock pretrained Surya | your **control** \u2014 the \"before\" |\n| **Stage 4** | 37 | your 32-step fine-tuned model | your **treatment** \u2014 the \"after\" |\n\nSame 32 samples both times (16 val from 2014\u201315, 16 test from 2023). Nothing else in the notebook ever shows val or test data to the model. Your instinct earlier was right.\n\n## Stage 1 does zero training\n\nYour own cell comment says it: *\"LoRA adapters start at zero contribution, so this really is stock Surya -- it is your control.\"*\n\nThat's the mechanism worth understanding, because it's what makes the comparison clean. LoRA computes `\u0394W = B\u00b7A`, and PEFT initializes `B` to **exactly zero**. So a freshly LoRA-wrapped model is *numerically identical* to plain pretrained Surya \u2014 not approximately, bit-for-bit. And `head_unembed` was copied from the pretrained `unembed`. So Stage 1 is pure inference on untouched pretrained weights: no gradient steps, no optimizer, no backward pass. It only loads data, predicts, and scores.\n\nThe reason it's slow is data, not compute \u2014 every sample is a cold S3 read, and it warms the cache for Stage 4.\n\n## Heads-up on Stage 4's last table\n\n`evaluate()` defaults to `paired=False` (confirmed in cell 13), so the final table \u2014 `flipped minus upright, per sample` \u2014 **will print all NaN**. Each sample gets scored in only one orientation, so there's nothing to subtract. Don't put that table in the presentation. Tables 1\u20133 are fine; they compare flipped samples against different upright samples, which is a weaker but real comparison.\n\n## Yes \u2014 pretrained-weight plots, and that's your problem-statement figure\n\nRight now `lit_model` holds the fine-tuned checkpoint, so 4b would give you \"after\" panels. Do it in this order:\n\n**1. Let Stage 4 finish, then run 4b as-is.** Cache is warm, weights are loaded \u2014 these are your \"after\" figures.\n\n**2. Rebuild stock Surya:** re-run cells **5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10**. That discards the fine-tuned weights and gives you pretrained again. Verify it worked:\n\n```python\nbs = [p for n, p in lit_model.named_parameters() if \"lora_B\" in n]\nmx = max(float(p.abs().max()) for p in bs)\nprint(f\"max|B| = {mx:.3e}  ->\", \"STOCK PRETRAINED\" if mx == 0 else \"still fine-tuned!\")\n```\n\nExpect `0.000e+00`. That's proof you're plotting the control.\n\n**3. Run 4b again \u2014 this is the figure you actually want:**\n\n```python\nreport_and_plot(test_ds, 0, \"test\", lit_model, orient=\"UPRIGHT\")\nreport_and_plot(test_ds, 0, \"test\", lit_model, orient=\"FLIPPED\")\n```\n\nSame `test_ds`, same index `0`, same 2023 flare, same pretrained weights \u2014 **the only thing that changes is orientation**. If flipping breaks stock Surya, that's the pair of figures that shows it, and it needs no fine-tuning at all to be a legitimate result. That is the problem you're trying to solve, stated visually.\n\n**4. To get the fine-tuned model back**, one line:\n\n```python\nckpt = torch.load(\"finetuned_32steps.ckpt\", map_location=\"cpu\", weights_only=False)\nlit_model.load_state_dict(ckpt[\"state_dict\"])\n```\n\nOne caveat so you're not surprised: at 32 steps the pretrained and fine-tuned panels will look nearly identical to the eye. The pretrained UPRIGHT-vs-FLIPPED pair is the figure with real signal in it \u2014 build the presentation around that one.Careful \u2014 one of those two will corrupt your results. Let me check what Stage 4b actually calls.",
